  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nThe morning started off with dense fog in northeast Nebraska and\nisolated weak thunderstorms near the Missouri borders of\nNebraska and southwest Iowa. Low ceilings remain in place north\nof the Platte River this afternoon as a cold front inches\nfarther into the forecast area. It is tied to a sfc low in far\nnortheast South Dakota. \n\nSoutherly winds have pushed temps into the 70s ahead of the\nfront, but dewpoints have trailed by 10-15 degrees which leaves\nexpected sfc based instability on the lower side (1400 J/kg or\nless). Forcing for ascent will be better across Minnesota and\ncloser to the low pressure center later this evening. Best \nchances across Nebraska and Iowa will be directly along the cold\nfront where forcing is maximized and instability peaks due to \nmoisture pooling ahead of the front. Shear is sufficient for \nlarge hail should any supercells develop, but the threat is \nconditional. In other words, likelihood of initiation is low \n(25%), but *should that happen*, the chance of severe weather \nis decent (20% threat of any such storm becoming severe). Put \nthat math together and you're looking at a 5% chance of severe \nweather at any given point in western Iowa and south of I-80 in\neastern Nebraska.\n\nThe front and storm chances will be out of the CWA by midnight.\n\nOvernight lows will range from lower 60s near Missouri to\nlower-40s closer to South Dakota. RH values may fall shy\nof the century mark overnight, but with light winds under the\nsfc high, wouldn't be surprised to see patchy fog \ndevelopment... especially anywhere impacted by rain earlier in \nthe evening. If temps slipped a little bit cooler than forecast,\nfog may need to be added to the grids.",
